        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "34ac78ca-2f8e-507f-8703-f043d70afe80",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: Year Books, early English law reports\n- People: Edward I of England, Roger de Bankwell\n- Roles: King of England, counsel, justice of the king's bench\n- Places: England, Gascony, Wales, Ireland, Scotland\n\nFacts:\n- The Year Books are the modern English name for the earliest law reports of England.\n- The Year Books survive as a continuous series from 1268 to 1535 and cover the reigns from Edward I to Henry VIII.\n- Edward I of England was born on 17/18 June 1239, became King of England in 1272, and reigned until his death on 7 July 1307.\n- Roger de Bankwell appeared as counsel in the Year Book for 1340.\n- Roger de Bankwell was appointed to a justiceship of the king's bench in 1341.\n- Roger de Bankwell was assigned between 1341 and 1347 to try petitions from Gascony, Wales, Ireland, Scotland, and other foreign parts.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "769220b4-e7f4-5354-803f-bc5ac836bdf0",
        "text": "Page: Year Books. Sentence: The Year Books are the modern English name that is now typically given to the earliest law reports of England.\nPage: Year Books. Sentence: They are extant in a continuous series from 1268 to 1535, covering the reigns of King Edward I to Henry VIII.\nPage: Edward I of England. Sentence: Edward I (17/18 June 1239 – 7 July 1307), also known as Edward Longshanks and the Hammer of the Scots (Latin: \"Malleus Scotorum\" ), was King of England from 1272 to 1307.\nPage: Roger de Bankwell. Sentence: He appears as a counsel in the Year book for 1340, in 1341 was appointed to a justiceship of the king's bench, and was one of those assigned to try petitions from Gascony, Wales, Ireland, Scotland, and 'other foreign parts ' between the years 1341 and 1347.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
